course: dictionary
lesson: 3094
title: Efficiency: doing more with less
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Efficiency means doing a job well without wasting time, energy, money or materials. Look at the clues around the word. «The shop worked with great efficiency, serving every customer quickly.» Quick service with no wasted effort is efficiency.
explain: In science, efficiency is a percentage. You work it out with useful energy out ÷ total energy in × 100%. A light bulb with high efficiency turns more of its electricity into light and less into heat, so more of its energy is useful.
explain: Be careful with words that sound good. «Efficiency savings» is a polite way of saying budget cuts, and «efficiency gains» often means fewer staff doing the same work. Ask yourself what is really happening, and look at the plain facts to see the real size of the change.
fact: Efficiency means doing a job well without wasting time, energy, money or materials.
fact: In science, efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in × 100%, given as a percentage.
fact: «Efficiency savings» means budget cuts in the UK.
fact: In the US, an efficiency is a very small flat or apartment with one main room.
fact: Useful phrases: energy efficiency, fuel efficiency, with great efficiency, improve efficiency.
exercise: «The shop worked with great efficiency, serving every customer quickly.» What does efficiency mean here? | A very small room with one window | Having lots of shops in one place | Losing money on every sale | *Doing a job well without wasting time
exercise: In science, a light bulb with high efficiency does what? | Uses no electricity at all | Makes lots of heat and very little light | *Turns more electricity into light and less into heat | Stops working after one day
exercise: «Efficiency gains» often means what? | The machines are painted new colours | Staff are given extra holidays | *Fewer staff doing the same work | More people are hired for the same work
test: An energy efficiency label tells you how well an appliance uses energy. What does a higher efficiency percentage mean? | *More of the energy you put in becomes useful work | The appliance is bigger | It needs no power at all | Less of the energy becomes useful work
test: Dad says, «We've got an efficiency in New York.» What is an efficiency? | A high score in a game | *A very small flat with one main room | A type of petrol | A large house with a garden
exam: A kettle has an efficiency of 90%. What does this mean? | The kettle costs 90 pounds | The kettle holds 90 litres | The kettle is 90 years old | *Nine tenths of the energy put in becomes useful work
